# Implementation plan for Project-Tensor: TensorCUDA ops for the Kokoro port (P1-T, items T1–T6)

**From:** Project-Voice (lead), 2026-10-12. **To:** Project-Tensor. **Authorized by David, 2026-10-12:** "You can
build your implementation plan that you need for TensorCUDA - Then you can MAIL it to Project-Tensor, It will build
what you need."

**Read with:** `research/kernels/TENSOR_REQUEST_P1T.md` (the request, with shapes from the shipped graph) and
`engine/tensor_requests/p1t_tests/` (the acceptance tests, the adapter contract `adapter_api.py`, and the stub adapter
`adapters/tensorcuda_stub.py`). All paths below are under `/mnt/ForgeRealm/Project-Voice/` unless they start with
`tensor_cuda/`, which means `/mnt/ForgeRealm/Project-Tensor/tensor_cuda/` (read on 2026-10-12 at main `9df5bef`).

## 1. Why, and what Project-Voice does with it

Kokoro-82M must run natively on TensorCUDA: Frontier allows no ONNX Runtime or PyTorch at runtime, and the voice
shares the RTX 4070 SUPER with GRAPA. Project-Voice owns the Kokoro-specific kernels (SineGen, the source STFT, the
iSTFT tail; done and parity-tested as P1-V1) and the model assembly (P1-V2). TensorCUDA owns the **generic** ops
(David's D2). Six are missing or semantically different today. With them, Project-Voice builds P1-V2: the full model
end to end on TensorCUDA, judged against the registered gates in `research/kernels/P1V2_GATES.md`.

## 2. Scope

| # | Deliverable | Python surface (suggested; your naming wins) | Unblocks |
|---|---|---|---|
| T1 | DLPack interop | `Tensor.__dlpack__`, `Tensor.__dlpack_device__`, `tensor_cuda.from_dlpack`, `Tensor.data_ptr` | Voice's own CUDA kernels reading and writing TensorCUDA memory |
| T2 | Conv1d: dilation, stride, asymmetric zero pads, groups | `functional.conv1d(x, w, b, stride, padding=(pb, pe), dilation, groups)` | Text encoder, predictor, decoder, generator resblocks |
| T3 | ConvTranspose1d, deterministic, groups, output_padding | `functional.conv_transpose1d(x, w, b, stride, padding, output_padding, dilation, groups)` | Generator upsamplers, depthwise pools |
| T4 | Bidirectional LSTM (ONNX semantics) | `functional.lstm(x, W, R, B, direction, hidden_size, initial_h, initial_c, layout)` | Text encoder, duration and prosody predictors (6 LSTMs) |
| T5 | InstanceNorm1d, LayerNorm (configurable eps/axis), GroupNorm fix | `functional.instance_norm1d`, `layer_norm(axis, eps)`, `group_norm` | 70 InstanceNorms, 31 LayerNorms |
| T6 | Nearest upsample ×k (1-D), reflection pad 1-D, `rint` | `functional.upsample_nearest1d`, `reflection_pad1d`, `rint` | F0/N upsampling, generator pad, duration rounding |

**Forward only.** Kokoro inference needs no autograd for any of these. If your house style wires autograd, keep it
out of the forward path's numerics.

**Out of scope:** SineGen, STFT/iSTFT, Snake, AdaIN application (Voice's), fast/persistent kernels (P2), quantized
paths, multi-stream support beyond what T1 needs.

## 3. Priority and milestones

Deliver in this order; each milestone is useful on its own. Mail Project-Voice at each one.

1. **M1 = T1** (DLPack). Smallest, and it lets Voice run its P1-V1 kernels on TensorCUDA tensors immediately.
2. **M2 = T2 + T3** (convolutions). Most of the model's FLOPs.
3. **M3 = T4** (BiLSTM). The hardest numerically.
4. **M4 = T5 + T6** (norms and small ops).

## 4. Numerics: the rules every op must keep

These are what keep P1-V2 inside its waveform gate (its budget assumes fp32 arithmetic near ORT's own reordering
floor, ≤ 1.6e-5).
- **fp32 math throughout.** No TF32, bf16 or fp16 compute shortcuts. `src/matmul.cu` calls
  `cublasSgemmStridedBatched` on a handle with the default math mode, which is not TF32; keep it that way (do not set
  `CUBLAS_TF32_TENSOR_OP_MATH`; the tests also fail if `NVIDIA_TF32_OVERRIDE=1` leaks in).
- **fp16 weight storage, fp32 math (D6).** When weights arrive as fp16 they are expanded to fp32 before use; the
  activations and accumulators are fp32.
- **Accurate transcendentals.** `expf`, `tanhf`, `logf` from the CUDA math library; no `__expf`/`__tanhf`
  intrinsics, no `--use_fast_math` for these translation units. A fast exp sits inside honest fp32 noise, so the
  tests cannot catch it; it is a code-review item.
- **Determinism.** Two runs on the same inputs must be bitwise equal. No `atomicAdd` in any reduction or scatter
  whose order can vary (this rules out reusing `col2im_kernel` in `src/conv.cu`, which accumulates with
  `atomicAdd`).
- **Statistics in fp32** for every norm, two-pass or Welford, biased variance (ONNX).

## 5. Per-op implementation notes

### T1: DLPack (`src/bindings.cpp`, `tensor_cuda/__init__.py`)
- Export: `__dlpack__(*, stream=None, max_version=None, dl_device=None, copy=None)` returns a PyCapsule named
  `"dltensor"` (or `"dltensor_versioned"` when `max_version >= (1, 0)`), zero-copy, with a deleter that holds a
  reference to the owning storage, so the memory outlives the producer's last Python reference.
- `__dlpack_device__()` returns `(2, device_ordinal)` (kDLCUDA).
- **Stream contract** (array API): `stream` is the consumer's stream. TensorCUDA queues all work on the legacy default
  stream. Unless `stream == -1`, record a `cudaEvent` on the legacy stream and `cudaStreamWaitEvent(consumer, event)`
  before returning (for `stream` None or 1, legacy-to-legacy needs nothing).
- Import: `from_dlpack(obj)` calls `obj.__dlpack__(stream=1)` (the legacy default stream, as a DLPack int), wraps the
  pointer without copying, and keeps the capsule's manager alive until the TensorCUDA tensor is freed. Reject
  non-contiguous strides, non-CUDA devices and dtypes other than fp32/fp16 with a clear error.
- `Tensor.data_ptr()` returns the device address of element 0.
- The adapter's `t1_empty/t1_fill/t1_read/t1_stream` map to existing allocation, fill and download calls;
  `t1_read` must synchronize only TensorCUDA's stream.

### T2: Conv1d (`src/conv.cu`, `nn.py:541-551`)
Today `Conv1D` is a height-1 `Conv2D` through `_C.im2col(x, kh, kw, sh, sw, ph, pw)` (`nn.py:361`), with no dilation
and symmetric pads only.
- **Simplest correct path:** a 1-D im2col kernel with `dilation` and separate `(pad_begin, pad_end)`, followed by the
  existing fp32 matmul. `col[(n, c, k), t] = x[n, c, t*stride - pb + k*dilation]` (zero outside).
- **Groups:** run one GEMM per group (strided-batched over groups) for `groups > 1`; a direct kernel for
  `groups == C` (depthwise) is fine too.
- **Output length:** `(T + pb + pe - dilation*(K-1) - 1) // stride + 1`.
- Shapes to support are in the request's T2 row (dilations 1/3/5, stride 6, kernel sizes 1–20).

### T3: ConvTranspose1d (`nn.py:410-434` is 2-D and atomic)
- **Use a gather formulation**, not scatter: each output element `y[n, o, t]` sums over the input positions and taps
  that land on it: for each tap `k`, `s = t + pb - k*dilation`; it contributes iff `s % stride == 0` and
  `0 <= s/stride < T_in`. One thread per output element, a fixed summation order, so it is deterministic.
- Weight layout is ONNX `[C_in, C_out/groups, K]`. Support `groups == C` (Kokoro's depthwise pools, stride 2,
  output_padding 1) and `output_padding`.
- **Output length:** `stride*(T-1) + output_padding + dilation*(K-1) + 1 - pb - pe`.
- A GEMM-plus-deterministic-col2im path is also acceptable if the col2im is a gather (no atomics).

### T4: BiLSTM (`nn.py:590-646` is forward-only, torch gate order, a Python loop)
- **Semantics: ONNX LSTM.** `x [T, B, I]` (layout 0); `W [D, 4H, I]`, `R [D, 4H, H]`, `B [D, 8H]` = `[Wb; Rb]`;
  gate order **i, o, f, c** (ONNX), not torch's i, f, g, o; activations sigmoid/tanh/tanh; no clip, no peepholes;
  `direction` forward / reverse / bidirectional (index 0 forward, 1 reverse). Returns `Y [T, D, B, H]`, `Y_h`, `Y_c`.
- **Suggested structure:**
  1. one GEMM for the input projection of all steps and both directions: `G_x = x · Wᵀ + Wb` (fp32);
  2. a host-side C++ loop over steps (not Python: the frame-level LSTM runs ~1,200 steps), each step a GEMM/GEMV
     `h_{t-1} · Rᵀ + Rb` for both directions (batched over D) and one fused cell kernel:
     `i=σ(..), o=σ(..), f=σ(..), c̃=tanh(..), c=f⊙c+i⊙c̃, h=o⊙tanh(c)` with accurate `expf`/`tanhf`;
  3. the reverse direction reads `G_x` at `T-1-t` and writes `Y[T-1-t, 1]`.
- H = 256 everywhere; sequences up to about 1,200 steps; batch 1. Correctness first: a persistent kernel is P2.
- Gate: normwise ≤ 2e-5 against fp64 (lane decision LD12, `tolerances_r2.json`).

### T5: Norms (`nn.py:124-137`, `517-538`)
- **InstanceNorm1d** on `[N, C, T]`: per `(n, c)` mean and biased variance over T, `eps` 1e-5, optional affine;
  fp32 statistics. One block per row with a deterministic tree reduction.
- **LayerNorm** over `axes [axis, rank)` with configurable `eps` (Kokoro uses 1e-5; ALBERT uses 1e-12). Today's
  composed LayerNorm rounds to fp16 between ops when given fp16; compute in fp32 regardless of input dtype.
- **GroupNorm:** fix the `reshape([N, G, -1])` failure (`nn.py:520`; it is a known failing test in your suite) and
  match torch/ONNX GroupNormalization-21 with per-channel affine.

### T6: Small ops (`src/kernels.cu`)
- **`upsample_nearest1d(x, scale)`**, integer scale (2 and 300 in Kokoro): `y[..., i] = x[..., i // scale]`. Exact.
- **`reflection_pad1d(x, (pb, pe))`**, ONNX reflect (Kokoro uses (1, 0)). Exact.
- **`rint(x)`**: round half to even (`rintf`/`nearbyintf` in the default rounding mode), sign of zero kept, ±Inf and
  NaN pass through. Exact. **Add it as a new op; do not change `round`:** `U_ROUND` is `roundf`
  (`src/kernels.cu:434`) and your quantizers depend on its half-away-from-zero behaviour (`kernels.cu:5358-5406`,
  `gemm_apa.cu:124`).

## 6. Acceptance: Project-Voice's tests

The tests are written, reviewed blind, and proven to pass an honest fp32 implementation and fail 21 realistic wrong
ones. Run them in your fork against your build:

```
cd /mnt/ForgeRealm/Project-Voice
flock -w 1800 /tmp/forge-gpu.lock env PYTHONPATH=<your fork>/tensor_cuda python3 \
    engine/tensor_requests/p1t_tests/run_p1t.py \
    --adapter engine/tensor_requests/p1t_tests/adapters/tensorcuda_stub.py \
    --items T1,T2,T3,T4,T5,T6 \
    --fixtures /mnt/ForgeRealm/Project-Voice/artifacts/p1t/fixtures \
    --tolerances engine/tensor_requests/p1t_tests/tolerances_r2.json \
    --out <your fork>/artifacts/p1t_report.json
```

- System `python3` has torch 2.11 (cu130) and numpy; torch is the fp64 oracle and the T1 peer.
- **The adapter `adapters/tensorcuda_stub.py` is a stub written from a read-only census.** Copy it into your fork,
  replace each `NotImplementedError` with your new call, and fix anything the census got wrong. Send us your adapter
  with the report; we will adopt it as the canonical TensorCUDA adapter.
- **Pass:** exit 0, every item PASS, with `tolerances_r2.json`. A run with any SKIP is PARTIAL (exit 3).
- Fixtures: 1.05 GB, read-only, hash-checked by the runner (`manifest.json` `89499fa5…`); real Kokoro weights and
  activations for two corpus cases (82 and 1,193 frames) plus synthetic edge cases.
- Wall time: T2–T6 about 80 s of CPU for the oracle plus your op time; T1 needs the GPU lease.
- Do not edit `tolerances*.json`, `knobs.py`, the fixtures, or the runner; the runner refuses changed tolerance files.
  If you believe a test is wrong, mail us the case and evidence instead.
- Your own suite: keep its known FAILED/ERROR baseline unchanged (52 ids, per your `27d87fd` build log), and add your
  usual regression tests for the new ops.

## 7. Process (your house rules)

- Project-Tensor is infra: work in `Project-Tensor/Fork/<name>`, reclaim, then promote with David's verbatim approval
  and a cross-model review receipt (house rules §10). Project-Voice does not touch your repo or your fork.
- GPU: take `/tmp/forge-gpu.lock` for every GPU process. Project-Voice's jobs use the same lock and will queue behind
  yours.
- A milestone may be delivered from the fork before promotion: send us the fork path and commit, and we will run our
  side read-only against it.

## 8. What to mail back (to `/mnt/ForgeRealm/SessionMail/Project-Voice/new/`)

At each milestone: the fork path and commit (and the main commit once promoted), the API names and signatures, the
`run_p1t.py` report JSON path with its verdict line, your adapter file, the build commands, and any deviation from
this plan with its reason.

## 9. Contact

