     2	// kernel. fp32 statistics, biased variance (ONNX), deterministic, no atomic operations, no fast-math flag.
     3	//
     4	// Every norm here is "normalize each contiguous row of a C-contiguous tensor over itself, then apply an affine":
     5	//   instance_norm1d  x [N, C, T]: rows (n, c), L = T,              scale/bias entry c            (NormAffine::kPerRow)
     6	//   layer_norm       x [d0..dr): rows prod(d0..d_{axis-1}), L = prod(d_axis..), entry j (scale/bias are x.shape[axis:])
     7	//   group_norm       x [N, C, T]: rows (n, g), L = (C/G) * T,       entry g*(C/G) + j/T (per channel, GroupNorm-21)
     8	//
     9	// Statistics, per row, fp32 (shifted two-pass):
    10	//   K    = x[row, 0]                                   (the shift: any sample of the row)
    11	//   d    = (sum_j (x_j - K)) / L                       (pass 1: the mean of the shifted row)
    12	//   var  = (sum_j ((x_j - K) - d)^2) / L               (pass 2: the centred sum of squares, biased)
    13	//   rstd = 1.0f / sqrtf(var + eps)                     (math-library sqrtf, IEEE-rounded; IEEE division)
    14	//   y_j  = fmaf(((x_j - K) - d) * rstd, scale, bias)   (pass 3)
    15	// Why the shift: the row mean itself is never formed in fp32. On a row with mean 1e4 and std 1e-2 (the order's
    16	// large-offset cell) the fp32 mean is off by up to half an ulp of 1e4 = 4.9e-4 = 0.05 std, which shifts every output
    17	// by 0.05 and fails the 1e-5 gate whatever the summation order (receipt: artifacts/p1t_m4/sim_large_offset.txt).
    18	// With the shift, x_j - K is exact on such rows (Sterbenz lemma: x_j and K within a factor 2), d is a small number
    19	// computed to full relative precision, and the output error scales with the row's spread, not with its offset.
    20	//
    21	// Reduction order (fixed, so two calls are bitwise equal): one block per row (grid-stride over rows), kNormBlock
    22	// threads. Thread t sums its terms j = t, t + B, t + 2B, ... in ascending order, kNormChunk terms at a time into a partial
    23	// sum that is then added to the thread total (blocked summation), and the B thread totals are combined by a fixed
    24	// shared-memory tree (pairs at stride B/2, B/4, ..., 1). The order depends only on L, kNormBlock and kNormChunk.
