     1	// tc_signal T6 small ops (P1T-M4): nearest upsample x k (1-D), reflection pad (1-D), rint. Bit-exact by construction:
     2	// the two gathers move 32-bit words (one load and one store per output element, through unsigned-integer pointers, so
     3	// NaN payloads, signed zeros and subnormals are copied bit for bit), and rint is the math library's rintf (IEEE 754
     4	// roundTiesToEven in the default rounding mode, sign of zero kept, NaN and +-Inf pass through). No fast-math flag, no
     5	// atomic operations: each output element is written by exactly one thread. TensorCUDA core's `round` (half away from
     6	// zero, used by its quantizers) is untouched; rint is a new op here.
     7	//
     8	// Semantics (the shipped Kokoro nodes):
     9	//  * upsample_nearest1d: ONNX Resize mode=nearest, coordinate_transformation_mode=asymmetric, nearest_mode=floor,
    10	//    scales [1, ..., 1, s] with s an integer >= 1: y[..., i] = x[..., floor(i / s)], i in [0, T*s). Integer division is
    11	//    the floor for these non-negative operands, so no float coordinate is ever formed.
    12	//  * reflection_pad1d: ONNX Pad mode=reflect on the last axis (= torch reflection_pad1d): with s = i - pad_begin,
    13	//    y[..., i] = x[..., |s|] for s < 0, x[..., 2(T-1) - s] for s >= T, else x[..., s]. pad_begin, pad_end < T.
    14	//  * rint: ONNX Round (half to even).
    15	//
    16	// Prior art: ONNX operator specs (Resize, Pad, Round); IEEE 754-2008 roundTiesToEven / C99 rint; torch's
    17	// reflection_pad1d and nearest upsample for the same index maps. Ours: nothing beyond the one-thread-per-output CUDA
    18	// mapping (no prior art known to me for anything else here).
